{
  "_note": "THE CIB&RC LABEL-CLAIM TABLE. India registers pesticides against a specific crop AND a specific pest. A molecule legal on tomato fruit borer is NOT legal on onion thrips unless separately claimed. In October 2017, 21 farmers died in Yavatmal; the Centre for Science and Environment's root-cause finding was that state advisories were recommending molecules with no CIB&RC label claim for the crop they were sprayed on. This table exists to make that failure structurally impossible in this app.",
  "_verification": "EVERY ROW IS status:'draft'. These are combinations commonly published in state IPM packages, transcribed here so the engine can be built and tested. Team task N1: verify each against PPQS 'Major Uses of Pesticides' (ppqs.gov.in/en/divisions/cib-rc/major-uses-of-pesticides) and the CIB&RC Registered Products list, then set status to 'cited' with the exact source and revision date. The API refuses to serve a draft row when strict=true.",
  "_toxicity_legend": "India's label triangle: red = extremely toxic, yellow = highly toxic, blue = moderately toxic, green = slightly toxic.",

  "restricted_maharashtra": {
    "_note": "Section 27 of the Insecticides Act lets a state prohibit sale, distribution or use in the public interest. Maharashtra used it in September 2018 (Extraordinary Gazette No. 183) for a 60-day ban on five formulations after 63 deaths statewide. A recommendation must be able to go stale overnight, so this list is data, never code.",
    "entries": [
